WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:10.020
Today we are going to use OpenAI’s&nbsp;
new language model AI to get it to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.940
write our homework essays for us, see&nbsp;
how well it does that, and get this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.940 --> 00:00:21.180
we will even ask it to write some computer&nbsp;
code for us. And it gets even better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.180 --> 00:00:28.620
it does all this for free! But not in the way&nbsp;
you think. You will find out in a moment why.

00:00:28.620 --> 00:00:35.580
Now, let’s see. First, we write, “When I&nbsp;
was young, I only liked to p”. And then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.580 --> 00:00:42.900
let’s say something comes here between, and&nbsp;
then, “and that’s how first I got interested in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:49.620
AI research.” Now we ask the AI to fill in the&nbsp;
middle part. Let’s see what happens together.

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:56.280
This indeed filled in the middle. It says,&nbsp;
when I was young, I only liked to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.280 --> 00:01:03.060
video games. I would play sometimes more&nbsp;
than 13 hours per day. The rush, novelty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.060 --> 00:01:09.900
and variety were beyond anything real life&nbsp;
could offer. (Whoa, hold it right there,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:16.260
little AI!) It continues - I loved the&nbsp;
challenge and I excelled at it. I would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.260 --> 00:01:22.260
often skip classes and go to and that’s&nbsp;
how first I got interested in AI research.

00:01:22.260 --> 00:01:29.040
But there is a problem. Did you notice the&nbsp;
problem? Yes, the video game addiction is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:34.260
kind of a problem. But, that’s not what&nbsp;
I meant. It gets worse. Grammatically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:40.560
it kind of connects to the suffix, the text&nbsp;
that comes after, it is not nearly perfect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:46.980
but, perhaps the worst thing is that there&nbsp;
is no logical connection between them. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:53.700
does not explain how we ended up being an AI&nbsp;
researcher. Although Demis Hassabis, the CEO of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:59.880
the DeepMind AI lab often says in interviews that&nbsp;
he played a ton of video games back in his day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:06.000
so this AI may be up to something. But&nbsp;
this new method can do way better. Look.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.420
“When I was young, I only liked to play video&nbsp;
games. Over time, I started thinking if it’d be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:20.100
possible to make bots to play better than any&nbsp;
human can ever play these games. I eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:26.100
decided I liked working on the latter more than&nbsp;
playing the games themselves and that’s how first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:33.060
I got interested in AI research.” Now that’s&nbsp;
a scholarly completion! Excellent. Loving it.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:38.640
And, it has more absolutely magical&nbsp;
capabilities. For instance, here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:44.460
we can start diving into our narrative&nbsp;
immediately by talking about Wikipedia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:50.220
and, look. If we give it some space&nbsp;
before it, the AI will recognize that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:56.460
telling the audience what Wikipedia is is&nbsp;
necessary, and it does it. Really cool!

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:02.880
And, if we feel even lazier, we can write&nbsp;
the start of the story, the end of the story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:09.360
and now, off you go little AI, and you&nbsp;
do the hard work. Let’s read it. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:15.600
commercial diver finally thought he’d snagged&nbsp;
a big catch when he saw something white. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.180
then he quickly realized it wasn’t a fish&nbsp;
− he was wrangling an alligator. That is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:27.300
a great story. Bravo. I made sure to look&nbsp;
it up, white alligators apparently exist.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:32.280
And it does not stop at writing&nbsp;
essays. Now hold on to your papers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.280 --> 00:03:37.080
because it can also write&nbsp;
computer code for us. Let’s see.

00:03:37.080 --> 00:03:44.580
It can finish our code that sorts a list, computes&nbsp;
the average of numbers, or even performs prime&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:52.260
factorization. But how does it do all this?&nbsp;
Is this some crazy mind-reading AI? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:59.760
not quite. It is very good, but it does not read&nbsp;
our thoughts. It reads our comments. These are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.760 --> 00:04:05.760
not part of the computer code, these comments are&nbsp;
a crutch to explain what the next block of code&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.760 --> 00:04:14.160
is about to do. And, with that, the AI knows how&nbsp;
it can help us out. This feels like magic. Just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:20.580
imagine how much easier this makes a bunch of&nbsp;
coding work for us. And what incredible speedup&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:27.120
in productivity we can expect from techniques&nbsp;
like this. Mind-blowing. What a time to be alive!

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:34.560
But wait, of course, not even this technique is&nbsp;
perfect. Sometimes it just does not know when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.560 --> 00:04:41.820
to stop. And sometimes, it just keeps repeating&nbsp;
itself. Alright, we get it, little AI. Thank you!

00:04:41.820 --> 00:04:47.460
And, there is one more feature that I loved&nbsp;
when reading this paper. You see, normally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.460 --> 00:04:53.460
you would need to train a separate neural network&nbsp;
for this fill in the middle capability. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.460 --> 00:04:58.860
the coolest thing here is that it can&nbsp;
generate continuous text from left to right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:05.460
just like the good old GPT-3 AI and&nbsp;
other variants. And, at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.460 --> 00:05:10.620
this can also fill in the middle&nbsp;
without being explicitly trained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.620 --> 00:05:17.400
to do that. Oh yes! That’s right - we get&nbsp;
this amazing capability essentially for free.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:21.300
So, what do you think? Does&nbsp;
this get your mind going? Let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.300 --> 00:05:24.960
me know in the comments below,&nbsp;
and let the experiments begin!

